 "title": "Why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3",
 "sources": [
  "0.30000000000000004.com ('Floating Point Math'): floating-point numbers are stored in binary; 0.1 and 0.2 'are repeating decimals in the base-2 system the computer uses', so 0.1 + 0.2 gives 0.30000000000000004; lists decimal types (Java BigDecimal, Python decimal, C# decimal) and exact fractions as options; approximate values 'can only be compared up to a given tolerance'. https://0.30000000000000004.com/",
  "MDN, Number.EPSILON: 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 is false; do not use === with floats; compare with Math.abs(x - y) < Number.EPSILON, which suits values around magnitude 1; for larger values (e.g. around 2000) a tolerance scaled like 2000 * Number.EPSILON is needed. https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/EPSILON",
  "Not stated by either source: 'use whole cents for money' is a common practice (integers are exact), not a quote; the source only lists decimal types. The '~0.1 / ~0.2' chips illustrate approximation, no digits claimed."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Add zero point one and zero point two in JavaScript, and you would expect zero point three.",
   "caps": "*ADD 0.1 AND 0.2 | EXPECT *0.3"
  },
  {
   "text": "You get zero point three, then fifteen zeros, then a four.",
   "caps": "0.3 | *15 ZEROS | THEN A *4"
  },
  {
   "text": "The reason is that computers store numbers in binary, where zero point one repeats forever.",
   "caps": "STORED IN *BINARY | 0.1 *REPEATS FOREVER"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the stored values are only approximate, and the tiny error shows up when you add.",
   "caps": "ONLY *APPROXIMATE | THE *ERROR SHOWS UP"
  },
  {
   "text": "That is why checking whether the sum equals zero point three is false.",
   "caps": "*EQUALS CHECK | IS *FALSE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Instead, compare with a small tolerance, such as JavaScript's Number dot epsilon.",
   "caps": "COMPARE WITH A *TOLERANCE | NUMBER.*EPSILON"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: that epsilon only suits numbers around one, so larger values need a bigger tolerance.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | SUITS NUMBERS AROUND *ONE | *BIGGER TOLERANCE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So never compare floats with equals, and for money, use whole cents or a decimal type.",
   "caps": "NEVER COMPARE *FLOATS | MONEY: WHOLE *CENTS | OR A *DECIMAL TYPE"
  }
 ],
